The porrectus at electa requires the alternate form in the greciliae font. Add the following lines in the gabc file header, prior to the %% and initial clef:
def-m1:\grechangeglyph{PorrectusLongqueue*}{*}{.alt};
def-m2:\greresetglyph{PorrectusLongqueue*};
The code [nm1] and [nm2] within the body of the chant then executes these macros to change the porrectus shape for this line and then to switch it back to normal.

Adding the TeX macros below just before the %% in the gabc file should change the format of the Torculus Resupinus at "et", but doesn't seem to be working now.
def-m1:\grechangeglyph{TorculusResupinus*}{*}{.alt};
def-m2:\greresetglyph{TorculusResupinus*};
%%

The score require the .alt form of the Torculus Resupinus at perfecta. To get that in TeX, add the lines:
def-m1: \grechangeglyph{TorculusResupinus*}{*}{.alt};
def-m2: \greresetglyph{TorculusResupinus*};
prior to the %% in the gabc file, or in the TeX file, add the grechangeglyph command before the gregorioscore command to load the score, and then the greresetglyph command afterwards.
This score is nearly identical to Score number 16408, Beata es Virgo Maria Dei Genetrix (for which the cantus ID is a match) on page 271*.

Score contains an alternate form of Porrectus at Angelus on line 1. I've inserted macro codes [nm1] and [nm2] in the gabc file that should provide this in TeX, but it will require the addition of the following lines prior to the %%:
def-m1:\grechangeglyph{Porrectus*}{*}{.alt};
def-m2:\greresetglyph{Porrectus*};
%%
Alternatively, the two lines can be added to the TeX file (without semicolons):
\grechangeglyph{Porrectus*}{*}{.alt}
\gregorioscore{...this gabc file...}
\greresetglyph{Porrectus*}

